rth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\\n- e204 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The evidence implies spending above $1 trillion, so it remains in rung 5. The higher projected cost per Rubin rack supports the top rung, while reported project delays and cancellations are a downside risk; the evidence does not show that those projects are all hyperscaler projects or quantify a reduction to 2027 hyperscaler spending large enough to move the total below $1 trillion.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\",\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's stated forecast that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in 2027; this reference is rung 5.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"More than $1 trillion in 2027, based on NVIDIA's forecast against about $650 billion planned for 2026—growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The forecast falls in rung 5, and the supplied delay evidence does not quantify a reduction to this aggregate forecast.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains above $1 trillion: that forecast compares with about $650 billion planned for 2026, implying growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The reported $130 billion of blocked or delayed projects is a meaningful downside risk, but it is the value of projects across the US—not a quantified reduction to 2027 spending by the largest AI hyperscalers—so it cannot be deducted dollar for dollar or shown to take the forecast below $1 trillion. Higher rack costs and Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance are supportive context, but neither independently quantifies 2027 aggregate spending. The NVIDIA segment-revenue dependency has no judged conclusion to constrain this estimate.\",\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied outcome remain above $1 trillion, but the current evidence adds the $130 billion valuation for delayed or blocked projects and Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance. These alter the supporting facts and downside context, without quantifying a sufficient 2027 hyperscaler-spending reduction to change the rung.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"judgement\": 16,\n  \"view\": \"v13\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n  \"request_as_sent\": \"Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\\\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 21 items]\\n- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion