at will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e256 [fact; Bloomberg (reported), via Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1 that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models; the article frames this as a possible sign that a major NVIDIA customer's GPU demand (Meta had planned to buy \\\"millions\\\" of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier in the year) may be shifting toward reselling spare capacity.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n- e259 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, attributed by the company to continued strong demand in the data center market, and Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; total backlog rose to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, driven by customer demand and added capacity.\\n    Quote: The March quarter revealed a lot in three data points. Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% \\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus (a 31.88% beat) and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $34.70–35.20 billion and adjusted EPS of $13.55–14.25 and set out a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS. CEO Duke Austin laid out the long game: a path to “more than doubling our adjuste\\n- e261 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke Austin estimated a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation and large-load demand, framing the opportunity behind the AI infrastructure build-out.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\\n- e262 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year with organic growth of 51%, diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus (a 54.44% beat), and backlog of $12.45 billion versus $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure represents roughly 45% of company revenue and management says demand still exceeds supply.\\n    Quote: The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still ex\\n- e263 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus (a 15.85% beat), record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $18.50–19.25 billion with EPS of $28.25–29.75; its U.S. Electrical Construction segment revenue grew 33.1% in Q1 2026 and Mechanical Construction added 28.8%.\\n    Quote: Three data points from Q1 2026 do the work. Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75. … Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while \\n- e264 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin, and EPS of $3.59 above consensus; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Then came the math. Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e275 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya argued that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have existed for years, yet Nvidia's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he also said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \\\"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\\\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e374 [third_party_view; TIKR.com (citing TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter); original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen (analyst Joshua Buchalter), cited by TIKR.com, said computing capacity is genuinely tight right now: rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, which the firm reads as demand outpacing supply. No figures or specific period are given.\\n    Quote: TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. … Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. … All of that points to demand outpacing supply.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four will spend more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 5. The most likely outcome remains at or above $1 trillion: the stated forecast implies growth of more than roughly 54% from the approximately $650 billion planned for 2026. Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance and strong data-center supplier demand support the scale of the buildout, but do not independently quantify 2027 spending. The roughly $130 billion of blocked or delayed US projects is a real downside risk, but it is not a quantified reduction to the largest hyperscalers' 2027 capex; Meta's possible shift toward selling compute is also not evidence of a sized cancellation. Neither therefore implies a shift below the rung-5 boundary. The NVIDIA segment-revenue depend\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\",\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's forecast that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in 2027; this reference is rung 5 ($1,000 billion or more).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"More than $1 trillion in 2027, implied by NVIDIA's forecast against roughly $650 billion planned for 2026—growth of at least about 54%. The other evidence does not quantify a different 2027 total.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains just over $1 trillion: that would be at least about 54% above the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. New supplier-demand and infrastructure-cost evidence is directionally supportive but does not quantify a different 2027 total. The roughly $130 billion of delayed or blocked projects and the possibility of Meta reselling compute are downside risks, but neither is a quantified reduction to these hyperscalers' 2027 spending. They do not move the forecast below the $1 trillion boundary. The NVIDIA revenue dependency is not yet judged and supplies no input; no themes are in force.\",\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied 2027 spending outcome remain consistent with the previous conclusion. The additional evidence is supportive or cautionary but does not provide a quantified revision to the forecast or alter the key implied figure.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"judgement\": 18,\n  \"view\": \"v13\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-12\",\n  \"request_as_sent\": \"Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\\\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 24 items]\\n- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e85 [party_statement; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027 (next year), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e204, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e52 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that local opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and about $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes the GPUs are not the driver of the rack cost increase.\\n    Quote: The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n- e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\n- e130 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management, on its most recent quarterly conference call, forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in calendar 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026, as reported by Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e150 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\\n- e165 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 US data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed so far in 2026, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware cited in the article, and polling in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\\n- e204 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e256 [fact; Bloomberg (reported), via Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1 that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models; the article frames this as a possible sign that a major NVIDIA customer's GPU demand (Meta had planned to buy \\\"millions\\\" of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier in the year) may be shifting toward reselling spare capacity.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n- e259 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, attributed by the company to continued strong demand in the data center market, and Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; total backlog rose to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, driven by customer demand and added capacity.\\n    Quote: The March quarter revealed a lot in three data points. Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% \\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus (a 31.88% beat) and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $34.70–35.20 billion and adjusted EPS of $13.55–14.25 and set out a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS. CEO Duke Austin laid out the long game: a path to “more than doubling our adjuste\\n- e261 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke Austin estimated a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation and large-load demand, framing the opportunity behind the AI infrastructure build-out.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\\n- e262 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year with organic growth of 51%, diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus (a 54.44% beat), and backlog of $12.45 billion versus $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure represents roughly 45% of company revenue and management says demand still exceeds supply.\\n    Quote: The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still ex\\n- e263 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus (a 15.85% beat), record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $18.50–19.25 billion with EPS of $28.25–29.75; its U.S. Electrical Construction segment revenue grew 33.1% in Q1 2026 and Mechanical Construction added 28.8%.\\n    Quote: Three data points from Q1 2026 do the work. Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75. … Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while \\n- e264 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin, and EPS of $3.59 above consensus; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Then came the math. Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e275 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya argued that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have existed for years, yet Nvidia's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he also said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \\\"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\\\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e374 [third_party_view; TIKR.com (citing TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter); original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen (analyst Joshua Buchalter), cited by TIKR.com, said computing capacity is genuinely tight right now: rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, which the firm reads as demand outpacing supply. No figures or specific period are given.\\n    Quote: TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. … Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. … All of that points to demand outpacing supply.\\n- e389 [party_statement; Fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in the prior quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e399 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source AWS (plan), reported by NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains just over $1 trillion: that would be at least about 54% above the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. New supplier-demand and infrastructure-cost evidence is directionally supportive but does not quantify a different 2027 total. The roughly $130 billion of delayed or blocked projects and the possibility of Meta reselling compute are downside risks, but neither is a quantified reduction to these hyperscalers' 2027 spending. They do not move the forecast below the $1 trillion boundary. The NVIDIA revenue dependency is not yet judged and supplies no input; no themes are in force.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"rung_label\": \"Below $700 billion\",\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s projection is that the big four hyperscalers’ 2027 data-center capex will exceed $1 trillion; that reference is rung 5.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"More than $1 trillion in 2027, using NVIDIA’s stated projection directly. Relative to the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026, that implies growth of more than about 54%; the additional AWS GPU plan supports demand but spans 2027–2028 and cannot be counted in full as 2027 capex.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast that the big four hyperscalers’ 2027 data-center capex will exceed $1 trillion, which is rung 5. The forecast remains above the $1 trillion boundary: the newer AWS GPU deployment plan and Google’s 2026 capex guidance add support, while project delays and possible Meta compute resale are risks but do not quantify a reduction large enough to move the implied total below $1 trillion. Rack-cost estimates and evidence of tight computing capacity also lean toward higher spending, but do not establish a different total. The NVIDIA revenue dependency v2 is not yet judged and supplies no input.\",\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied total remain the same, but newer evidence adds Google’s $190 billion 2026 capex guidance and AWS’s plan for 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across 2027–2028 as supporting facts; the AWS plan spans two years and does not specify capex.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"judgement\": 19,\n  \"view\": \"v13\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-13\",\n  \"request_as_sent\": \"Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\\\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 27 items]\\n- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e85 [party_statement; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027 (next year), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e204, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e52 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that local opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and about $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes the GPUs are not the driver of the rack cost increase.\\n    Quote: The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n- e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\n- e130 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management, on its most recent quarterly conference call, forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in calendar 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026, as reported by Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e150 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\\n- e165 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 US data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed so far in 2026, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware cited in the article, and polling in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\\n- e204 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e256 [fact; Bloomberg (reported), via Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1 that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models; the article frames this as a possible sign that a major NVIDIA customer's GPU demand (Meta had planned to buy \\\"millions\\\" of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier in the year) may be shifting toward reselling spare capacity.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e259 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, attributed by the company to continued strong demand in the data center market, and Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; total backlog rose to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, driven by customer demand and added capacity.\\n    Quote: The March quarter revealed a lot in three data points. Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% \\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus (a 31.88% beat) and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $34.70–35.20 billion and adjusted EPS of $13.55–14.25 and set out a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS. CEO Duke Austin laid out the long game: a path to “more than doubling our adjuste\\n- e261 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke Austin estimated a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation and large-load demand, framing the opportunity behind the AI infrastructure build-out.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\\n- e262 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year with organic growth of 51%, diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus (a 54.44% beat), and backlog of $12.45 billion versus $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure represents roughly 45% of company revenue and management says demand still exceeds supply.\\n    Quote: The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still ex\\n- e263 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus (a 15.85% beat), record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $18.50–19.25 billion with EPS of $28.25–29.75; its U.S. Electrical Construction segment revenue grew 33.1% in Q1 2026 and Mechanical Construction added 28.8%.\\n    Quote: Three data points from Q1 2026 do the work. Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75. … Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while \\n- e264 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin, and EPS of $3.59 above consensus; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Then came the math. Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e275 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya argued that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have existed for years, yet Nvidia's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he also said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \\\"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\\\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e374 [third_party_view; TIKR.com (citing TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter); original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen (analyst Joshua Buchalter), cited by TIKR.com, said computing capacity is genuinely tight right now: rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, which the firm reads as demand outpacing supply. No figures or specific period are given.\\n    Quote: TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. … Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. … All of that points to demand outpacing supply.\\n- e389 [party_statement; Fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in the prior quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e399 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source AWS (plan), reported by NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n- e466 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to a range of $125 billion to $145 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion\\n- e467 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms launched Meta Compute, a service that rents its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises, a step 24/7 Wall St. says converts part of its AI capex into a recurring revenue stream.\\n    Quote: then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e256; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e469 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year (calendar 2027), adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, versus roughly 6.7 GW the four added in 2025; Google alone will add 6.8 GW.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast that the big four hyperscalers’ 2027 data-center capex will exceed $1 trillion, which is rung 5. The forecast remains above the $1 trillion boundary: the newer AWS GPU deployment plan and Google’s 2026 capex guidance add support, while project delays and possible Meta compute resale are risks but do not quantify a reduction large enough to move the implied total below $1 trillion. Rack-cost estimates and evidence of tight computing capacity also lean toward higher spending, but do not establish a different total. The NVIDIA revenue dependency v2 is not yet judged and supplies no input.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\",\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA’s forecast that the big four hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in calendar 2027; this reference is rung 5. Morgan Stanley’s forecast of over $1 trillion is for broader AI infrastructure, not precisely the same spending category.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Likely above $1 trillion in calendar 2027, placing combined data-center capex on rung 5. The direct NVIDIA forecast is reinforced by Morgan Stanley’s over-$1-trillion 2027 infrastructure forecast and its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, plus the AWS deployment plan and demand indicators; these do not support a more precise total.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which is rung 5. That forecast is not the sole basis for the judgement: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion of 2027 AI-infrastructure spending by Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta, while its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, the AWS deployment plan, and evidence of strong infrastructure demand support a high spending run-rate. The Morgan Stanley figure covers broader AI infrastructure, so it is corroboration rather than a direct dollar-for-dollar measure of data-center capex. Project delays and Meta’s excess-capacity offering are downward risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to put likely data-center capex below $1 trillion; the implied outcome remains above the rung-5 boundary.\",\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting evidence and implied outlook have changed: the newly supplied Morgan Stanley 2027 forecast and capacity estimate add quantified corroboration, while Meta’s raised 2026 capex plan and the reported launch of its excess-capacity service update the balance of support and risk. Project-delay reporting also adds a quantified downside risk.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"judgement\": 20,\n  \"view\": \"v13\",\n  \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n  \"request_as_sent\": \"Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\\\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 30 items]\\n- e84 [third_party_view; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e213; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e85 [party_statement; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027 (next year), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e130, e204, e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e52 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that local opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\\n- e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and about $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes the GPUs are not the driver of the rack cost increase.\\n    Quote: The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\\n- e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\\n- e130 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management, on its most recent quarterly conference call, forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in calendar 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026, as reported by Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e150 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\\n- e162 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\\n- e165 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 US data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed so far in 2026, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware cited in the article, and polling in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built near their home.\\n    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\\n- e204 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e213 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\\n- e256 [fact; Bloomberg (reported), via Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1 that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models; the article frames this as a possible sign that a major NVIDIA customer's GPU demand (Meta had planned to buy \\\"millions\\\" of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier in the year) may be shifting toward reselling spare capacity.\\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e467; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e259 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, attributed by the company to continued strong demand in the data center market, and Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; total backlog rose to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, driven by customer demand and added capacity.\\n    Quote: The March quarter revealed a lot in three data points. Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% \\n- e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus (a 31.88% beat) and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $34.70–35.20 billion and adjusted EPS of $13.55–14.25 and set out a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS. CEO Duke Austin laid out the long game: a path to “more than doubling our adjuste\\n- e261 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke Austin estimated a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation and large-load demand, framing the opportunity behind the AI infrastructure build-out.\\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\\n- e262 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year with organic growth of 51%, diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus (a 54.44% beat), and backlog of $12.45 billion versus $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure represents roughly 45% of company revenue and management says demand still exceeds supply.\\n    Quote: The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still ex\\n- e263 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus (a 15.85% beat), record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $18.50–19.25 billion with EPS of $28.25–29.75; its U.S. Electrical Construction segment revenue grew 33.1% in Q1 2026 and Mechanical Construction added 28.8%.\\n    Quote: Three data points from Q1 2026 do the work. Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75. … Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while \\n- e264 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin, and EPS of $3.59 above consensus; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n    Quote: Then came the math. Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\\n- e275 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya argued that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have existed for years, yet Nvidia's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he also said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \\\"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\\\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e374 [third_party_view; TIKR.com (citing TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter); original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen (analyst Joshua Buchalter), cited by TIKR.com, said computing capacity is genuinely tight right now: rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, which the firm reads as demand outpacing supply. No figures or specific period are given.\\n    Quote: TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. … Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. … All of that points to demand outpacing supply.\\n- e389 [party_statement; Fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in the prior quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), according to Fool.com.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e399 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source AWS (plan), reported by NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\\n- e466 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to a range of $125 billion to $145 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion\\n- e467 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms launched Meta Compute, a service that rents its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises, a step 24/7 Wall St. says converts part of its AI capex into a recurring revenue stream.\\n    Quote: then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e256; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e469 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year (calendar 2027), adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, versus roughly 6.7 GW the four added in 2025; Google alone will add 6.8 GW.\\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\\n- e474 [third_party_view; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as cited by the Motley Fool's Justin Pope. The figure is a broad AI-compute-spending aggregate, not the four hyperscalers' disclosed data center capital expenditure.\\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\\n- e501 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta has a $145 billion infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon, cited as a competitive threat to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\\n- e537 [fact; ebc.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt, as Wall Street demands proof of return on hyperscaler AI capital expenditure, according to the EBC article.\\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt .\\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which is rung 5. That forecast is not the sole basis for the judgement: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion of 2027 AI-infrastructure spending by Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta, while its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, the AWS deployment plan, and evidence of strong infrastructure demand support a high spending run-rate. The Morgan Stanley figure covers broader AI infrastructure, so it is corroboration rather than a direct dollar-for-dollar measure of data-center capex. Project delays and Meta’s excess-capacity offering are downward risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to put likely data-center capex below $1 trillion; the impli\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"rung_label\": \"Below $700 billion\",\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's forecast that the big four AI hyperscalers' 2027 data-center capital expenditures could reach or top $1 trillion is the reference; it is rung 5. This is a company forecast, rather than a broad market-consensus figure.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Combined 2027 spending is most likely at or above $1 trillion, implying rung 5, based primarily on NVIDIA's direct forecast. Morgan Stanley's over-$1-trillion 2027 AI-infrastructure forecast is broader than data-center capex, while the reported project delays and Meta capacity monetization are not quantified as 2027 reductions to this total.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four's 2027 data-center capital expenditures could reach or exceed $1 trillion, which sits in rung 5; it is a company forecast, not a broad consensus estimate. The evidence still implies spending at or above that threshold: the NVIDIA forecast is direct, and Morgan Stanley's forecast of over $1 trillion is supportive but covers broader AI infrastructure. The $130 billion of projects reported delayed or blocked is not all 2027 spending, and no evidence quantifies a reduction to the hyperscalers' 2027 total sufficient to move it below $1 trillion. Meta's excess-capacity service is a downside risk, but its effect on future capex is also unquantified.\",\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting and offsetting facts have materially changed: the evidence now includes AWS's plan for 2 million additional GPUs across 2027–2028, Google's and Meta's updated 2026 capex figures, a quantified $130 billion value for delayed or blocked projects, and Meta's excess-capacity service. These change the key facts and risks behind the forecast without establishing a quantified reduction below the rung-5 threshold.\"\n  }\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
