ed a new business model, pitched in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as a 'revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' under which partner AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream). Per Bloomberg, developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, and neither NVIDIA nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it\\u2019ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the \\u201crevenue-sharing and credit-support model\\u201d as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e34 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement under NVIDIA's new model runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n- e35 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions (to Digital Alpha and under the NVIDIA model).\\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\\n- e36 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that\\u2019s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\\n- e58 [fact; mitrade.com (citing NVIDIA's X/Twitter post of 2 July 2026); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new AI compute framework under which cloud providers deploy advanced NVIDIA hardware through revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements, aimed at lowering infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers and regional AI operators; NVIDIA earns revenue both from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by the supported capacity. The framework was announced by NVIDIA in an X/Twitter post dated 2 July 2026 and reported by mitrade.com; no amount of credit support or revenue-sharing terms was disclosed.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers. \\u2026 NVIDIA\\u2019s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and region\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e59 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support AI compute initiative, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The item gives no timing, contract value or credit-support amount for the deployment.\\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e60 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new AI compute initiative, is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity. No timeline, contract value or credit-support amount is given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled on July 1, 2026 a financing vehicle called the \\\"AI Compute Partnership\\\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, while taking a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. NVIDIA described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\"; the revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and NVIDIA declined to provide further details.\\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . \\u2026 Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e80 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI, an Australian sovereign-cloud provider and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA; the item also describes it as planning that deployment.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . \\u2026 Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e81 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to NVIDIA's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . \\u2026 Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] \\u2026 Firmus Techn\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e89 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] NVIDIA launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers under which AI cloud operators can procure NVIDIA infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold on that capacity; NVIDIA receives its standard product revenue from the infrastructure sales plus a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers. \\u2026 Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services s\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e90 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the model.\\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e91 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e103 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Analysts at Seaport Research published a note (referenced as appearing 'last week' in a barchart.com article dated 5 July 2026) raising concerns about a 'circular-financing boom': they argued that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips \\u2014 directly via equity or debt, or by allowing financing of these increasingly expensive chips \\u2014 the more it could mean the demand investors see is not real to the extent Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, and could lead to issues around earnings quality. The same note also flagged that Nvidia's balance sheet has 'growing issues' with 'plenty of turbulence underneath the surface.'\\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. \\u2026 That said, concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by \\n\\n[Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \\n\\n[Themes in force, 1]\\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-04; reasons: The stated reference is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellation\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is not placeable on the ladder: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount. The new evidence makes the backstop more concrete, including a six-year Sharon AI agreement and NVIDIA's fixed-rate rent-back of unused GPUs, but the two deployments' GPU capacity is not itself a dollar commitment. As a rough scale proxy, Sharon AI's separate Digital Alpha facility is up to $200 million for its 40,000-GPU deployment; scaling that by the combined 210,000-GPU capacity of Sharon AI and Firmus gives about $1.05 billion. This is not an NVIDIA commitment and the capacity is targeted rather than fully established as outstanding, so it supports an estimate below $5 billion, not a higher rung.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 4,\n   \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\",\n   \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the aggregate outstanding amount is supplied, so the market reference cannot be assigned to a ladder rung.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $31 billion: the $30 billion OpenAI participation in e37, plus an approximately $1.05 billion rough backstop proxy derived by scaling Sharon AI's $200 million facility for 40,000 GPUs (e35) to the 210,000-GPU capacity described in e2 and e14. The xAI financing amount is $20 billion overall, but NVIDIA's portion is unstated and is not counted at face value.\",\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is not placeable on the ladder: the supplied evidence gives no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan for the total outstanding amount. The best estimate is about $31 billion: e37 identifies NVIDIA's $30 billion OpenAI participation, while the new capacity-backstop program scales to roughly $1.05 billion using Sharon AI's $200 million facility for 40,000 GPUs as a rough comparator across the 210,000-GPU initial capacity. That is above the $20 billion rung-4 floor but below $40 billion; the xAI financing is $20 billion in total, but NVIDIA's share is not specified, so it cannot be added in full. The estimate is uncertain because the evidence does not establish which funded investments will be included in the reported outstanding total at FY2027 year-end.\",\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The prior conclusion relied on a roughly $1.05 billion capacity-based proxy and stayed below $5 billion. The newly considered e37 supplies a specific $30 billion NVIDIA participation in OpenAI financing, materially changing the implied scale and moving the forecast to rung 4.\"\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nJUDGEMENT\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"][x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"].find(\\\"[Current conclusions\\\"):],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nJUDGEMENT\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"][x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"].find(\\\"[Current conclusions\\\"):],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nJUDGEMENT 1 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated dollar target for outstanding commitments is supplied; therefore there is no reference amount or reference rung to locate.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Most likely below $5 billion at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). This is a qualitative base-case estimate for the limited initial cohort and contingent support model; the evidence supplies no commitment values, pricing, or terms from which to calculate a more precise total.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference for this question is supplied, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The evidence confirms a new credit-support/capacity-backstop model and names two initial participants, but does not disclose commitment amounts or terms. I forecast the initial, contingent programme exposure outstanding at FY2027 year-end to remain below $5 billion: the disclosed GPU deployments are plans or capacity targets, not evidence that all that capacity is committed or that NVIDIA backs its full value. This is a low-confidence base case, not a conclusion that the commitments are zero.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"First judgement.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 2 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference for this question is supplied, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The evidence confirms a new credit-support/capacity-backstop model and names two initial participants, but does not disclose commitment amounts or terms. I forecast the initial, contingent programme exposure outstanding at FY2027 year-end to remain below $5 billion: the disclosed GPU deployments are plans or capacity targets, not evidence that all that capacity is committed or that NVIDIA backs its full value. This is a low-confidence base case, not a conclusion that the commitments are zero.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference is supplied; therefore no reference rung can be assigned.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Below $5 billion at FY2027 year-end is the low-confidence base case. The disclosed 40,000-GPU Sharon agreement and Firmus's much larger planned capacity establish meaningful potential exposure, but the evidence gives neither the backstop amount or price nor proof that NVIDIA guarantees the full planned capacity; the implied figure is therefore an estimate below the rung-2 boundary, not a reported or independently quantified amount.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference is supplied, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. My base-case forecast remains below $5 billion: the evidence now includes a six-year Sharon AI agreement covering up to 40,000 GPUs and reports that Firmus adopted the backstop, but the disclosed deployments are capacity plans, not evidence that NVIDIA guarantees the full value of all that capacity. The backstop applies to unused capacity at a pre-agreed price, and the amount, price, and expected unsold share are not disclosed. I therefore forecast exposure from the initial, contingent cohort below the rung-2 threshold, with low confidence; the evidence does not establish that the amount is zero.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but the key supporting facts have changed: e34 adds disclosed terms for Sharon AI's six-year, 72 MW agreement, and e28 reports Firmus's adoption of the backstop. These sharpen the scale and existence of the initial programme compared with the prior conclusion, without disclosing a dollar commitment that would support a different rung.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 3 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No consensus, company guidance, or other dollar reference is supplied, so the reference cannot be placed on the ladder. My base-case forecast remains below $5 billion: the evidence now includes a six-year Sharon AI agreement covering up to 40,000 GPUs and reports that Firmus adopted the backstop, but the disclosed deployments are capacity plans, not evidence that NVIDIA guarantees the full value of all that capacity. The backstop applies to unused capacity at a pre-agreed price, and the amount, price, and expected unsold share are not disclosed. I therefore forecast exposure from the initial, contingent cohort below the rung-2 threshold, with low confidence; the evidence does not establish that the amount is zero.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the total is supplied; the reference is therefore unplaceable on the ladder.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast: below $5 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end (rung 1). The announced backstop has no disclosed dollar cap or utilization assumptions, so the announced GPU capacity cannot be converted into an outstanding commitment; the $30 billion OpenAI participation is described as funding already provided, not an outstanding year-end commitment.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the reported total is supplied, so the market reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The new program and its initial deployments increase the potential exposure, but the disclosed GPU and MW capacities are not dollar commitments, and NVIDIA's backstop amount, pricing, and expected unused capacity remain unspecified. I forecast the outstanding total below $5 billion rather than counting the full value of the planned deployments. The $30 billion OpenAI participation is a significant financing fact, but it is described as cash already funnelled into a funding round, not as an outstanding commitment at FY2027 year-end; the separate $200 million Digital Alpha facility is not an NVIDIA commitment. The evidence therefore does not support moving across the $5 billion boundary, though the forecast remains uncertain.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the supporting facts have changed: the evidence now includes a six-year Sharon AI agreement covering 72 MW and up to 40,000 GPUs, Firmus's reported adoption and planned capacity, and NVIDIA's $30 billion OpenAI funding-round participation. These facts alter the exposure assessment, although they do not establish an outstanding NVIDIA commitment above the rung-2 threshold at FY2027 year-end.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 4 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 1]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-04; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The stated reference is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellation\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the reported total is supplied, so the market reference cannot be placed on the ladder. The new program and its initial deployments increase the potential exposure, but the disclosed GPU and MW capacities are not dollar commitments, and NVIDIA's backstop amount, pricing, and expected unused capacity remain unspecified. I forecast the outstanding total below $5 billion rather than counting the full value of the planned deployments. The $30 billion OpenAI participation is a significant financing fact, but it is described as cash already funnelled into a funding round, not as an outstanding commitment at FY2027 year-end; the separate $200 million Digital Alpha facility is not an NVIDIA commitment. The evidence therefore does not support movin\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for NVIDIA's total outstanding financing commitments and capacity backstops is supplied, so the market reference cannot be assigned a rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"A rough scale proxy is about $1.05 billion: the separate $200 million Digital Alpha facility disclosed for Sharon AI's 40,000-GPU deployment, scaled to the two partners' combined 210,000-GPU stated capacity. Because this is not an NVIDIA commitment and the capacity is targeted, the estimate remains below the $5 billion rung boundary.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is not placeable on the ladder: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount. The new evidence makes the backstop more concrete, including a six-year Sharon AI agreement and NVIDIA's fixed-rate rent-back of unused GPUs, but the two deployments' GPU capacity is not itself a dollar commitment. As a rough scale proxy, Sharon AI's separate Digital Alpha facility is up to $200 million for its 40,000-GPU deployment; scaling that by the combined 210,000-GPU capacity of Sharon AI and Firmus gives about $1.05 billion. This is not an NVIDIA commitment and the capacity is targeted rather than fully established as outstanding, so it supports an estimate below $5 billion, not a higher rung.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the six-year, 72 MW Sharon AI agreement and the disclosed $200 million comparable facility allow a rough capacity-scaled estimate of about $1.05 billion for the two initial deployments, changing the key supporting figure from an unquantified exposure.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 5 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 1]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-04; reasons: The stated reference is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellation\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is not placeable on the ladder: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount. The new evidence makes the backstop more concrete, including a six-year Sharon AI agreement and NVIDIA's fixed-rate rent-back of unused GPUs, but the two deployments' GPU capacity is not itself a dollar commitment. As a rough scale proxy, Sharon AI's separate Digital Alpha facility is up to $200 million for its 40,000-GPU deployment; scaling that by the combined 210,000-GPU capacity of Sharon AI and Firmus gives about $1.05 billion. This is not an NVIDIA commitment and the capacity is targeted rather than fully established as outstanding, so it supports an estimate below $5 billion, not a higher rung.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated plan for the aggregate outstanding amount is supplied, so the market reference cannot be assigned to a ladder rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $31 billion: the $30 billion OpenAI participation in e37, plus an approximately $1.05 billion rough backstop proxy derived by scaling Sharon AI's $200 million facility for 40,000 GPUs (e35) to the 210,000-GPU capacity described in e2 and e14. The xAI financing amount is $20 billion overall, but NVIDIA's portion is unstated and is not counted at face value.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is not placeable on the ladder: the supplied evidence gives no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan for the total outstanding amount. The best estimate is about $31 billion: e37 identifies NVIDIA's $30 billion OpenAI participation, while the new capacity-backstop program scales to roughly $1.05 billion using Sharon AI's $200 million facility for 40,000 GPUs as a rough comparator across the 210,000-GPU initial capacity. That is above the $20 billion rung-4 floor but below $40 billion; the xAI financing is $20 billion in total, but NVIDIA's share is not specified, so it cannot be added in full. The estimate is uncertain because the evidence does not establish which funded investments will be included in the reported outstanding total at FY2027 year-end.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The prior conclusion relied on a roughly $1.05 billion capacity-based proxy and stayed below $5 billion. The newly considered e37 supplies a specific $30 billion NVIDIA participation in OpenAI financing, materially changing the implied scale and moving the forecast to rung 4.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 6 \n Thesis v9 (NVDA, asset, focus \"NVIDIA's financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners at FY2027 year-end\", FY2027, due 2027-02-28): What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: The reported total is below $5 billion. / rung 2 Bullish: The reported total is at least $5 billion and below $10 billion. / rung 3 Neutral: The reported total is at least $10 billion and below $20 billion. / rung 4 Bearish: The reported total is at least $20 billion and below $40 billion. / rung 5 Strongly bearish: The reported total is at least $40 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 1]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-06; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The evidence implies spending above $1 trillion, so it remains in rung 5. The higher projected cost per Rubin rack supports the top rung, while reported project delays and cancellations