orize imports by\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: There is no stated company-wide total, consensus figure, or guidance for this question, so the reference has no rung. The evidence implies about $26.5 billion: use Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop as the anchor, and size Firmus's announced backstop using its 170,000-GPU project and SemiAnalysis's lower-end $2.36/GPU-hour rate over six years (about $21.1 billion); then add NVIDIA's roughly $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment. Firmus's announced project and reported committed off-take support using its stated scale, although execution remains a risk; the estimate excludes any unquantified datacenter-lease backstops. The estimate is within rung 4's $20–40 billion range, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No company-wide total, consensus, or guidance is supplied for this question; there is therefore no reference rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $26.5 billion in quantified commitments: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI, approximately $21.1 billion for Firmus based on the lower-end $2.36/GPU-hour estimate over six years for 170,000 GPUs, and approximately $0.5 billion of Firmus preference shares. The backstops span six years and are outstanding commitments, not annualized amounts; additional datacenter-lease backstops are disclosed but unquantified.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no company-wide total, consensus figure, or guidance for this question, so the reference has no rung. The quantified estimate is about $26.5 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, plus an estimated $21.1 billion for Firmus using 170,000 GPUs × $2.36 per GPU-hour × 8,760 hours × six years, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment. This is within rung 4's $20–40 billion range, so it does not cross a boundary. SemiAnalysis also says NVIDIA has begun backstopping datacenter leases, but gives no amount; that adds unquantified exposure without supporting a more precise total. The dependency v1 has no judged conclusion to constrain this estimate.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung and quantified estimate remain approximately $26.5 billion, but e113 adds a material new fact not reflected in the prior estimate: NVIDIA has also begun backstopping datacenter leases. Its unquantified size changes the key scope of the forecast, though it does not establish a different rung.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 13 \n Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 5, \"rung_lab