e is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellations weigh against it but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied total below $1 trillion.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 5, \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management's stated 2027 forecast is that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion, following a projected roughly $650 billion in 2026. That reference is rung 5.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"NVIDIA's forecast implies 2027 combined spending above $1 trillion, which is rung 5. The roughly $650 billion 2026 plan is the base, not a flat annualized run-rate; the supplied evidence gives no quantified net adjustment to NVIDIA's 2027 forecast.\", \"reasons\": \"The evidence implies spending above $1 trillion, so it remains in rung 5. The higher projected cost per Rubin rack supports the top rung, while reported project delays and cancellations are a downside risk; the evidence does not show that those projects are all hyperscaler projects or quantify a reduction to 2027 hyperscaler spending large enough to move the total below $1 trillion.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting facts have changed: the new Morgan Stanley rack-cost estimate supports higher capex per unit, while the quantified tally of blocked or delayed projects adds a more specific downside risk. Neither supplies a net quantified revision to 2027 hyperscaler spending.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 15 \n Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest