: \"Likely above $1 trillion in calendar 2027, placing combined data-center capex on rung 5. The direct NVIDIA forecast is reinforced by Morgan Stanley’s over-$1-trillion 2027 infrastructure forecast and its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, plus the AWS deployment plan and demand indicators; these do not support a more precise total.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which is rung 5. That forecast is not the sole basis for the judgement: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion of 2027 AI-infrastructure spending by Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta, while its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, the AWS deployment plan, and evidence of strong infrastructure demand support a high spending run-rate. The Morgan Stanley figure covers broader AI infrastructure, so it is corroboration rather than a direct dollar-for-dollar measure of data-center capex. Project delays and Meta’s excess-capacity offering are downward risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to put likely data-center capex below $1 trillion; the implied outcome remains above the rung-5 boundary.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting evidence and implied outlook have changed: the newly supplied Morgan Stanley 2027 forecast and capacity estimate add quantified corroboration, while Meta’s raised 2026 capex plan and the reported launch of its excess-capacity service update the balance of support and risk. Project-delay reporting also adds a quantified downside risk.\"}\n\nJUDGEMENT 20 \n Thesis v13 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"Combined data-center capital expenditures of the largest AI hyperscalers in calendar 2027\", 2027, due 2028-12-31): How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Below $700 billion: Combined spending is less than $700 billion. / rung 2 $700 billion to below $800 billion: Combined spending is at least $700 billion and less than $800 billion. / rung 3 $800 billion to below $900 billion: Combined spending is at least $800 billion and less than $900 billion. / rung 4 $900 billion to below $1000 billion: Combined spending is at least $900 billion and less than $1000 billion. / rung 5 $1000 billion or more: Combined spending is at least $1000 billion. \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast of more than $1 trillion in 2027 data-center capex by the big four hyperscalers, which is rung 5. That forecast is not the sole basis for the judgement: Morgan Stanley also forecasts over $1 trillion of 2027 AI-infrastructure spending by Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta, while its 19.5 GW capacity-addition estimate, the AWS deployment plan, and evidence of strong infrastructure demand support a high spending run-rate. The Morgan Stanley figure covers broader AI infrastructure, so it is corroboration rather than a direct dollar-for-dollar measure of data-center capex. Project delays and Meta’s excess-capacity offering are downward risks, but the evidence does not quantify a reduction sufficient to put likely data-center capex below $1 trillion; the impli\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \nANSWER {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Below $700 billion\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's forecast that the big four AI hyperscalers' 2027 data-center capital expenditures could reach or top $1 trillion is the reference; it is rung 5. This is a company forecast, rather than a broad market-consensus figure.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Combined 2027 spending is most likely at or above $1 trillion, implying rung 5, based primarily on NVIDIA's direct forecast. Morgan Stanley's over-$1-trillion 2027 AI-infrastructure forecast is broader than data-center capex, while the reported project delays and Meta capacity monetization are not quantified as 2027 reductions to this total.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four's 2027 data-center capital expenditures could reach or exceed $1 trillion, which sits in rung 5; it is a company forecast, not a broad consensus estimate. The evidence still implies spending at or above that threshold: the NVIDIA forecast is direct, and Morgan Stanley's forecast of over $1 trillion is supportive but covers broader AI infrastructure. The $130 billion of projects reported delayed or blocked is not all 2027 spending, and no evidence quantifies a reduction to the hyperscalers' 2027 total sufficient to move it below $1 trillion. Meta's excess-capacity service is a downside risk, but its effect on future capex is also unquantified.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting and offsetting facts have materially changed: the evidence now includes AWS's plan for 2 million additional GPUs across 2027–2028, Google's and Meta's updated 2026 capex figures, a quantified $130 billion value for delayed or blocked projects, and Meta's excess-capacity service. These change the key facts and risks behind the forecast without establishing a quantified reduction below the rung-5 threshold.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
