{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"][x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"].find(\\\"[Current conclusions\\\"):],\\\"\\\\n\\\",json.dumps(x[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for x in p[5:14]]'\"","aggregated_output":"\n 6 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 1]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-06; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The evidence implies spending above $1 trillion, so it remains in rung 5. The higher projected cost per Rubin rack supports the top rung, while reported project delays and cancellations are a downside risk; the evidence does not show that those projects are all hyperscaler projects or quantify a red\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is not placeable on the ladder: the supplied evidence gives no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan for the total outstanding amount. The best estimate is about $31 billion: e37 identifies NVIDIA's $30 billion OpenAI participation, while the new capacity-backstop program scales to roughly $1.05 billion using Sharon AI's $200 million facility for 40,000 GPUs as a rough comparator across the 210,000-GPU initial capacity. That is above the $20 billion rung-4 floor but below $40 billion; the xAI financing is $20 billion in total, but NVIDIA's share is not specified, so it cannot be added in full. The estimate is uncertain because the evidence does not establish which funded investments will be included in the reported outstanding total at FY2027 year-end.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan for the total outstanding amount is supplied. The reference is therefore unplaceable on the ladder.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $25.6 billion nominal: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion backstop plus an estimated $20.74 billion for Firmus, scaled from the same programme's disclosed Sharon deal by GPU capacity (170,000/40,000). Firmus's stated capacity is a target, so the estimate is uncertain, but both deals have six-year terms and the evidence supports a total above $20 billion at FY2027 year-end.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is not placeable: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The evidence implies about $25.6 billion of nominal backstops: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop, and scaling that same-programme deal by Firmus's 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 gives about $20.74 billion for Firmus. Firmus's capacity is a target rather than fully demonstrated volume, so the estimate is uncertain, but the announced backstop and six-year term support a total above $20 billion; it remains below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The $25–30 billion of Firmus customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the past OpenAI funding participation does not establish an amount still outstanding at FY2027 year-end. This stays at rung 4, but the key supporting figure has changed from the previous rough estimate.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but new evidence quantifies Sharon AI's backstop at $4.88 billion and supports sizing Firmus's announced backstop by comparison, replacing the previous rough estimate with an approximately $25.6 billion nominal estimate.\"}\n\n 7 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 1]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-07; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains above $1 trillion: that forecast compares with about $650 billion planned for 2026, implying growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The reported $130 billion of blocked or dela\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: The reference is not placeable: no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives a total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The evidence implies about $25.6 billion of nominal backstops: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop, and scaling that same-programme deal by Firmus's 170,000 GPUs versus Sharon's 40,000 gives about $20.74 billion for Firmus. Firmus's capacity is a target rather than fully demonstrated volume, so the estimate is uncertain, but the announced backstop and six-year term support a total above $20 billion; it remains below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The $25–30 billion of Firmus customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the past OpenAI funding participation does not establish an amount still outstanding at FY20\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan supplies a total outstanding commitment amount; the reference is not placeable on the ladder.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $29.3 billion outstanding at FY2027 year-end: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $24.4 billion for Firmus, scaling the disclosed Sharon backstop by Firmus's 360 MW versus Sharon's 72 MW. The six-year term and June 2026 announcement mean the Sharon commitment is still within term at the due date; the estimate assumes the scaled Firmus commitment is likewise outstanding.\", \"reasons\": \"No consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives the total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The best quantified estimate is about $29.3 billion: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop for 72 MW, and Firmus's 360 MW project implies about $24.4 billion when scaled at the same amount per MW; together these are below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The six-year Sharon commitment remains within its term at FY2027 year-end. Firmus's $25–30 billion of customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the additional datacenter-lease backstops are unquantified, so neither is added to the estimate.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 4, but the implied total changes from about $25.6 billion to about $29.3 billion: the new 72 MW Sharon deal disclosure supports scaling Firmus by its 360 MW capacity rather than by the earlier GPU-count comparison. The revised estimate remains below $40 billion.\"}\n\n 8 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 2]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-08; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four will spend more than $1 trillion in 2027, which sits at rung 5. The most likely outcome remains at or above $1 trillion: the stated forecast implies growth of more than roughly 54% from the approximately $650 billion planned for 2026. Google's $19\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-08; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The observable pre-report reference is no public authorization, which is rung 2; no separate market consensus or company guidance is supplied. The specific, recent report that officials told Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek they will be permitted to buy limited H200 quantities makes authorization by \n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: No consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan gives the total outstanding amount, so the reference has no ladder rung. The best quantified estimate is about $29.3 billion: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop for 72 MW, and Firmus's 360 MW project implies about $24.4 billion when scaled at the same amount per MW; together these are below the $40 billion boundary for rung 5. The six-year Sharon commitment remains within its term at FY2027 year-end. Firmus's $25–30 billion of customer off-take is not itself NVIDIA's backstop, and the additional datacenter-lease backstops are unquantified, so neither is added to the estimate.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No supplied consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated plan quantifies the FY2027 year-end total; the reference is therefore unquantified and has no rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $25.6 billion outstanding: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI plus an estimated $20.7 billion for Firmus, calculated by scaling Sharon's disclosed backstop per GPU to Firmus's 170,000 GPUs. The estimate is within rung 4's $20–40 billion boundaries.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is unquantified: no consensus, company guidance, or stated plan gives NVIDIA's total outstanding amount, so it has no rung. The best quantified forecast is about $25.6 billion: Sharon AI disclosed a $4.88 billion six-year backstop for up to 40,000 GPUs; applying that amount per GPU to Firmus's 170,000-GPU project gives about $20.7 billion, for a combined estimate of about $25.6 billion. Both projects are within the relevant multi-year period at FY2027 year-end, and the estimate remains within rung 4's $20–40 billion range. Firmus's $25–30 billion is customer off-take revenue, not NVIDIA's backstop amount, so it is not added. NVIDIA's reported $500 million Firmus preference-share investment and its unquantified datacenter-lease backstops are additional exposure indicators, but the evidence does not establish that either amount belongs in the outstanding capacity-backstop total at the due date.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 4, but the implied total is revised from the earlier roughly $29.3 billion estimate: the Firmus comparable is now scaled by its disclosed 170,000 GPUs against Sharon AI's 40,000 GPUs, rather than by megawatts. The newly reported $500 million Firmus preference-share investment is noted separately and is not treated as a capacity backstop.\"}\n\n 9 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 2]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-