pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No consensus, company guidance, or stated NVIDIA plan gives a total outstanding amount; the reference is unquantified and has no rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $26.1 billion at FY2027 year-end: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop scaled from 40,000 GPUs to Firmus's 170,000 GPUs implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus, or about $25.6 billion across both backstops; adding the disclosed $500 million Firmus preference-share financing gives about $26.1 billion.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated company-wide total, so the reference has no rung. The implied estimate is about $26.1 billion, within rung 4's $20–40 billion range: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, scaled by the 170,000-GPU Firmus project versus Sharon's 40,000 GPUs, implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus and $25.6 billion combined; adding NVIDIA's disclosed $500 million Firmus preference-share financing gives about $26.1 billion. This remains below the rung 5 threshold of $40 billion. The amount for Firmus is a comparable-based estimate, not a disclosed backstop value; Firmus's $25–30 billion is customer off-take revenue and is not added as NVIDIA exposure.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 4, but the supporting implied total changes from about $25.6 billion to about $26.1 billion by adding the newly disclosed $500 million Firmus preference-share financing commitment.\"}\n\n 10 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 2]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-12; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast that the big four hyperscalers’ 2027 data-center capex will exceed $1 trillion, which is rung 5. The forecast remains above the $1 trillion boundary: the newer AWS GPU deployment plan and Google’s 2026 capex guidance add support, while project delays and possible M\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-12; reasons: The confirmed-status-quo reference is rung 2: the supplied reporting says Chinese authorities had not yet allowed H200 imports. The reported plan to permit selected companies to buy limited quantities points to rung 1, and the deadline leaves time for the stated individual-approval process to produc\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: There is no consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated company-wide total, so the reference has no rung. The implied estimate is about $26.1 billion, within rung 4's $20–40 billion range: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, scaled by the 170,000-GPU Firmus project versus Sharon's 40,000 GPUs, implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus and $25.6 billion combined; adding NVIDIA's disclosed $500 million Firmus preference-share financing gives about $26.1 billion. This remains below the rung 5 threshold of $40 billion. The amount for Firmus is a comparable-based estimate, not a disclosed backstop value; Firmus's $25–30 billion is customer off-take revenue and is not added as NVIDIA exposure.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 1, \"rung_label\": \"Strongly bullish\", \"reference\": \"No market consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated company-wide total is supplied; there is no reference rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Approximately $26.1 billion outstanding: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI, about $20.7 billion for Firmus by scaling Sharon's disclosed backstop by the 170,000-to-40,000 GPU capacity ratio, plus about $0.5 billion of Firmus preference shares.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no company-wide consensus, NVIDIA guidance, or stated total for this question, so the reference has no rung. The implied estimate is about $26.1 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, scaled by Firmus's stated 170,000-GPU capacity versus Sharon's 40,000 GPUs, implies about $20.7 billion for Firmus; adding Sharon's $4.88 billion and NVIDIA's roughly $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment gives about $26.1 billion. Firmus's $25–30 billion of expected customer revenue supports the scale of the project but is not itself NVIDIA's backstop. The estimate is in rung 4 ($20–40 billion), below rung 5's $40 billion boundary. Firmus's stated capacity is a project target rather than fully demonstrated deployed volume, which tempers confidence in the comparable-based estimate; the announced backstop and committed off-take nevertheless support a substantial commitment.\", \"changed\": false, \"why_changed\": \"The rung and the key estimate remain the previous conclusion's rung 4 and approximately $26.1 billion. The newer Firmus off-take disclosure provides context for the announced project but is customer revenue, not a new NVIDIA backstop amount, so it does not change the estimate.\"}\n\n 11 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 2]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-13; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s forecast of more than