dance is supplied for the reported outstanding amount; the reference has no rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $26.5 billion: Sharon AI's $4.88 billion disclosed backstop, plus about $21.1 billion for Firmus (170,000 GPUs × six years × 8,760 hours/year × $2.36/GPU-hour), plus NVIDIA's roughly $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no stated company-wide total, consensus figure, or guidance for this question, so the reference has no rung. The evidence implies about $26.5 billion: use Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop as the anchor, and size Firmus's announced backstop using its 170,000-GPU project and SemiAnalysis's lower-end $2.36/GPU-hour rate over six years (about $21.1 billion); then add NVIDIA's roughly $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment. Firmus's announced project and reported committed off-take support using its stated scale, although execution remains a risk; the estimate excludes any unquantified datacenter-lease backstops. The estimate is within rung 4's $20–40 billion range, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The SemiAnalysis pricing detail supplies a new basis for sizing Firmus: applying its $2.36/GPU-hour lower-end rate to the six-year, 170,000-GPU project gives about $21.1 billion, refining the previous Firmus estimate of about $20.7 billion. Adding the same Sharon AI and Firmus equity amounts yields about $26.5 billion rather than $26.1 billion; the rung remains 4.\"}\n\n 12 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v1 (open; last_end) What will Nvidia's total revenue be in FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Themes in force, 2]\n- v13 (ai-capex, due 2028-12-31) How much will the largest AI hyperscalers spend on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2027?: now rung 5 $1000 billion or more; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's forecast that the big four's 2027 data-center capital expenditures could reach or exceed $1 trillion, which sits in rung 5; it is a company forecast, not a broad consensus estimate. The evidence still implies spending at or above that threshold: the NVIDIA forecast is direc\n- v8 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will Chinese authorities issue any authorization for Chinese companies to import Nvidia H200 chips by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-07-14; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the presently reported status quo—no publicly confirmed Chinese import authorization—which sits at rung 2. Nvidia's reported uncertainty and zero-China-revenue guidance are consistent with that baseline, but they are not a direct forecast of whether Beijing will authorize imports by\n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 4; reasons: There is no stated company-wide total, consensus figure, or guidance for this question, so the reference has no rung. The evidence implies about $26.5 billion: use Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop as the anchor, and size Firmus's announced backstop using its 170,000-GPU project and SemiAnalysis's lower-end $2.36/GPU-hour rate over six years (about $21.1 billion); then add NVIDIA's roughly $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment. Firmus's announced project and reported committed off-take support using its stated scale, although execution remains a risk; the estimate excludes any unquantified datacenter-lease backstops. The estimate is within rung 4's $20–40 billion range, so it does not cross a rung boundary.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views. \n {\"rung\": 4, \"rung_label\": \"Bearish\", \"reference\": \"No company-wide total, consensus, or guidance is supplied for this question; there is therefore no reference rung.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $26.5 billion in quantified commitments: $4.88 billion for Sharon AI, approximately $21.1 billion for Firmus based on the lower-end $2.36/GPU-hour estimate over six years for 170,000 GPUs, and approximately $0.5 billion of Firmus preference shares. The backstops span six years and are outstanding commitments, not annualized amounts; additional datacenter-lease backstops are disclosed but unquantified.\", \"reasons\": \"There is no company-wide total, consensus figure, or guidance for this question, so the reference has no rung. The quantified estimate is about $26.5 billion: Sharon AI's disclosed $4.88 billion six-year backstop, plus an estimated $21.1 billion for Firmus using 170,000 GPUs × $2.36 per GPU-hour × 8,760 hours × six years, plus NVIDIA's approximately $0.5 billion Firmus preference-share commitment. This is within rung 4's $20–40 billion range, so it does not cross a boundary. SemiAnalysis also says NVIDIA has begun backstopping datacenter leases, but gives no amount; that adds unquantified exposure without supporting a more precise total. The dependency v1 has no judged conclusion to constrain this estimate.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung and quantified estimate remain approximately $26.5 billion, but e113 adds a material new fact not reflected in the prior estimate: NVIDIA has also begun backstopping datacenter leases. Its unquantified size changes the key scope of the forecast, though it does not establish a different rung.\"}\n\n 13 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion] none: this is the first judgement, so changed must be true.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \n {\"rung\": 5, \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\", \"reference\": \"Nvidia's projection that the big four AI hyperscalers' 2027 data-center capex could reach $1 trillion; that reference is rung 5.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $1 trillion in calendar 2027, based on Nvidia's specific projection, compared with the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026. The 2026 project delays or cancellations provide a downward risk but no spend amount from which to quantify a lower total.\", \"reasons\": \"The stated reference is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellations weigh against it but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied total below $1 trillion.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"This is the first judgement.\"}\n\n 14 \n [Current conclusions of the theses it depends on]\n- v2 (open; last_end) What Data Center segment revenue will NVIDIA report for FY2027?: now rung - not judged yet; reasons: \n\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The stated reference is Nvidia's projection that the big four could reach $1 trillion in 2027, which is rung 5. The evidence therefore supports a forecast at the top rung; the 2026 plan of about $650 billion shows the scale of the projected increase, while the reported project delays or cancellations weigh against it but do not quantify a reduction sufficient to move the implied total below $1 trillion.\n\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views only the dependency thesis ids listed above. \n {\"rung\": 5, \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management's stated 2027 forecast is that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion, following a projected roughly $650 billion in 2026. That reference is rung 5.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"NVIDIA's forecast implies 2027 combined spending above $1 trillion, which is rung 5. The roughly $650 billion 2026 plan is the base, not a flat annualized run-rate; the supplied evidence gives no quantified net adjustment to NVIDIA's 2027 forecast.\", \"reasons\": \"The evidence implies spending above $1 trillion, so it remains in rung 5. The higher projected cost per Rubin rack supports the top rung, while reported project delays and cancellations are a downside risk; the evidence does not show that those projects are all hyperscaler projects or quantify a reduction to 2027 hyperscaler spending large enough to move the total below $1 trillion.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting facts have changed: the new Morgan Stanley rack-cost estimate supports higher capex per unit, while the quantified tally of blocked or delayed projects adds a more specific downside risk. Neither supplies a net quantified revision to 2027 hyperscaler spending.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
