 e33 e34 e35 e36 e37 e58 e59 e60 e111 e112 e113 e114 e115 e149 e243 e251 e252 e253 e285 e299\n13 e84 e85 e52\n14 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165\n15 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219\n16 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219 e256 e259 e260 e261 e262 e263 e264 e275\n17 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219 e256 e259 e260 e261 e262 e263 e264 e275 e374\n18 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219 e256 e259 e260 e261 e262 e263 e264 e275 e374 e389 e399\n19 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219 e256 e259 e260 e261 e262 e263 e264 e275 e374 e389 e399 e466 e467 e469\n20 e84 e85 e52 e119 e120 e122 e130 e150 e162 e165 e204 e213 e219 e256 e259 e260 e261 e262 e263 e264 e275 e374 e389 e399 e466 e467 e469 e474 e501 e537\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); seen=set();\nfor x in p[:12]:\n for m in re.finditer(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]):\n  k=m.group(1)\n  if k not in seen: seen.add(k); print(\\\"J\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],m.group(0))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); seen=set();\nfor x in p[:12]:\n for m in re.finditer(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]):\n  k=m.group(1)\n  if k not in seen: seen.add(k); print(\\\"J\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],m.group(0))'\"","aggregated_output":"J 1 - e1 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-07-01; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a new business model under which AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity, aligning the parties through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. … This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-pow\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 1 - e2 [party_statement; NVIDIA Corporation; original source Firmus Technologies ; from nvblog:nvidia-unlocks-ai-compute-at-scale-capital-partners-to-power-ai-infrastructure-buildout 2026-07-01] Firmus Technologies is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14, e15; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 1 - e12 [fact; The Information (relayed by moomoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] On 1 July 2026 NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, under which AI cloud providers can procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure and NVIDIA earns a share of cloud service revenue in addition to standard product revenue. The Information reported the same day that NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud providers that lease its GPUs — if they fail to secure enough AI developers to rent the capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price — in return for a percentage of those providers' revenue that gradually declines over the contract term. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program; three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement and an NVIDIA spokesperson confirmed the program's existence.\n    Quote: On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price. … In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud provider\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 1 - e14 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support model, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 1 - e15 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus, one of the first cloud providers participating in NVIDIA's new model, is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 1 - e18 [party_statement; Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO, blog post); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cdd5ccd6fd57b79e703bfb7d 2026-07-01] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, in a blog post, said the company will grant token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales, and will connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, establishing a new recurring 'usage-linked earnings stream' for NVIDIA. The revenue-sharing model targets researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources; under the branding 'DSX AI factories', an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider like Firmus Technologies faster than going through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\n    Quote: Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales. … The company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \"usagelinked earnings stream\" for itself, chief financial of\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 2 - e20 [fact; qz.com (citing an NVIDIA blog post by Kress); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday that gives AI startups, model builders and enterprises access to NVIDIA computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. Under the arrangement, revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to NVIDIA as AI cloud companies provide NVIDIA-powered services to their end customers; NVIDIA's Kress, in a blog post, called the resulting income a 'recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.' Token credits are also part of the offering.\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves. … Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end custo\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e21 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as one of two initial partners in its revenue-sharing program; Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. Sharon AI cofounder and CEO James Manning called the collaboration a 'pivotal moment' in the company's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure.\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. … \"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e29, e59; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e22 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-01; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Firmus Technologies as the second initial partner in its revenue-sharing program; a campus Firmus is developing in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. Firmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield said AI-native companies need scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally.\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. … \"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e23 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia announced a partnership program that uses its network of AI cloud service providers to give fast-growing AI startups access to critical computing infrastructure. Under the program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia. Nvidia named two initial Australian partners; the two are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia.\n    Quote: Two initial partners are offering potential access to more than 200,000 GPUs, including via an upcoming data center in Batam, Indonesia. … Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday. … C\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e24 [fact; CNBC; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, one of the two initial Australian AI-cloud partners Nvidia named for the program, will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e25 [fact; CNBC; original source Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies, an AI infrastructure company and the other initial Australian partner, says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e27 [party_statement; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said its new revenue-sharing and credit-support model lets AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity; under the model NVIDIA acts as a financial backstop and agrees to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate.\n    Quote: Nvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. … In a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, a\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e28 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Firmus is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia.\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Firmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e29 [fact; Georgia Butler, datacenterdynamics.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0 2026-07-02] Sharon AI is among the first companies to adopt NVIDIA's backstop offer and is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer. … Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e33 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new business model, pitched in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as a 'revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' under which partner AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services; NVIDIA collects its usual hardware product revenue plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity (a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream). Per Bloomberg, developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, and neither NVIDIA nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e34 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that its agreement under NVIDIA's new model runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to NVIDIA's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n\nJ 2 - e35 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions (to Digital Alpha and under the NVIDIA model).\n    Quote: The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions.\n\nJ 2 - e36 [party_statement; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that is expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e37 [fact; Tom's Hardware (Luke James); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing, arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\n    Quote: Nvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism.\n\nJ 2 - e58 [fact; mitrade.com (citing NVIDIA's X/Twitter post of 2 July 2026); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a new AI compute framework under which cloud providers deploy advanced NVIDIA hardware through revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements, aimed at lowering infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers and regional AI operators; NVIDIA earns revenue both from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by the supported capacity. The framework was announced by NVIDIA in an X/Twitter post dated 2 July 2026 and reported by mitrade.com; no amount of credit support or revenue-sharing terms was disclosed.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers. … NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and region\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e59 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing/credit-support AI compute initiative, plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The item gives no timing, contract value or credit-support amount for the deployment.\n    Quote: Early participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 2 - e60 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus, cited as an early participant in NVIDIA's new AI compute initiative, is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity. No timeline, contract value or credit-support amount is given.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 3 - e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled on July 1, 2026 a financing vehicle called the \"AI Compute Partnership\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, while taking a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. NVIDIA described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\"; the revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and NVIDIA declined to provide further details.\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\" in a blog post co-\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 3 - e80 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI, an Australian sovereign-cloud provider and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement with NVIDIA; the item also describes it as planning that deployment.\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 3 - e81 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies, a private company and one of the first two adopters of NVIDIA's AI Compute Partnership, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to NVIDIA's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture, expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] . … Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3] … Firmus Techn\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 4 - e89 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] NVIDIA launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers under which AI cloud operators can procure NVIDIA infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold on that capacity; NVIDIA receives its standard product revenue from the infrastructure sales plus a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity.\n    Quote: Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers. … Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services s\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 4 - e90 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Sharon AI, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the model.\n    Quote: Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 4 - e91 [party_statement; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus, an early adopter of NVIDIA's new revenue-sharing financing structure, is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 5 - e103 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source Seaport Research ; from gnews:ff7d99387d1dae92ede72145 2026-07-05] Analysts at Seaport Research published a note (referenced as appearing 'last week' in a barchart.com article dated 5 July 2026) raising concerns about a 'circular-financing boom': they argued that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips — directly via equity or debt, or by allowing financing of these increasingly expensive chips — the more it could mean the demand investors see is not real to the extent Nvidia's earnings releases suggest, and could lead to issues around earnings quality. The same note also flagged that Nvidia's balance sheet has 'growing issues' with 'plenty of turbulence underneath the surface.'\n    Quote: Analysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors. … That said, concerns around a \"circular-financing boom\" put forward by \n\n\nJ 6 - e110 [fact; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] The item notes Nvidia is 'fresh off launching' a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, referring to it as a recently completed step with no financial terms, participants, amounts or date disclosed.\n    Quote: The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\n\nJ 6 - e111 [fact; SemiAnalysis (newsletter.semianalysis.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that Nvidia runs a backstop program in which it gives Neoclouds a take-or-pay commitment — a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity — and in return shares in a portion of the Neocloud's revenue earned above the backstop level; lenders rely on Nvidia's backstop and its AA/Aa2 investment-grade credit rating, lending to match the length of the backstop.\n    Quote: In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level. … Capital: Lenders look to Nvidia’s backstop and its AA/Aa2 investment grade credit rating and are satisfied to lend matching the length \n\nJ 6 - e112 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis describes Nvidia's backstop program as typically six years long, during which Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over time. It presents an illustrative backstop pricing curve on the lower end of the range averaging $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, expects most Neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops, and estimates Nvidia's take rate averages about 18% over six years in its short-tenor rental scenario.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. … we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate h\n\nJ 6 - e113 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that Nvidia, beyond backstopping GPU rental offtakes, has begun backstopping datacenter leases.\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\n\nJ 6 - e114 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI's 72MW AI factory in Australia, announced in June 2026, scales to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year Nvidia backstop; SharonAI disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B (an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over six years). Its footprint is set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and it guides to more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total, including the 40,000 backstopped GB300s, by mid-2027.\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. … Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the compa\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 6 - e115 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus's 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia, announced on June 29, 2026, is a major project announced with an Nvidia backstop; Firmus announced it expects $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years, a portion of which (above the backstop level) SemiAnalysis expects to be shared with Nvidia.\n    Quote: Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. … Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six\n\nJ 6 - e146 [fact; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced a business model on 1 July 2026 under which AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support: participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services, while NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. According to the analyst, NVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue-share percentage or how usage is measured.\n    Quote: The News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of th\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 6 - e147 [fact; Futurum Group; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia as one of the first partners under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 6 - e148 [fact; Futurum Group; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus is building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, as one of the first partners under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, and is co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA's DSX blueprints.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners. … Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 6 - e149 [third_party_view; Futurum Group; original source Firmus ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Futurum Group writes that Firmus, one of the first partners building a DSX-aligned AI factory under NVIDIA's 1 July 2026 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, and flags as an open question whether that converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\n    Quote: Whether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\n\n\nJ 7 - e200 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corp. 2026-07-01; from gnews:ce5daf57bdca5da657e5f677 2026-07-07] On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA disclosed a profit-sharing program giving model builders, AI startups and businesses access to NVIDIA's computing framework without needing to fund hardware buildouts; under the arrangement, NVIDIA will receive, in addition to product revenue, a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to their end users. CFO Colette Kress framed the income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream that removes the need for location selection, building, energy procurement and hardware installation to access hyperscale computing. The company said the model intensifies engagement compared with ownership stakes in cloud firms such as Nebius and CoreWeave.\n    Quote: On July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers de\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 8 - e243 [fact; Australian Financial Review (Street Talk column); original source Nvidia 2026-07-08; from gnews:91a2f447977f812557cf0f6c 2026-07-08] Nvidia has emerged as one of the biggest investors in Singapore-based AI neocloud Firmus Technologies' US$2 billion (A$2.9 billion) equity raising, putting up its hand for about US$500 million (A$721 million) of Firmus preference shares that are designed to convert to ordinary equity at the initial public offering later this year. The raise is set to almost double Firmus's equity value to US$15.5 billion before its ASX listing, per the Australian Financial Review's Street Talk column.\n    Quote: Nasdaq-listed Nvidia has emerged as one of the biggest investors in Firmus Technologies’ $US2 billion ($2.9 billion) equity raising, which is set to almost double the neocloud’s equity value to $15.5 billion before its ASX listing. … Street Talk can reveal that the $US4.9 trillion chip developer has put up its hand for about $US500 million ($721 million) worth of Firmus’ preference shares, which a\n\nJ 8 - e251 [party_statement; Colette Kress and Raj Mirpuri (NVIDIA announcement blog), reported by Futuriom; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA announced a new business model under which it will guarantee the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (neoclouds) over several years and share in the cloud revenues earned above the guaranteed (backstop) level. In a joint announcement blog, CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri said the model lets AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers 'through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model,' with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of cloud revenue on the supported capacity, providing a 'recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.'\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares tumbled on recent news that the vendor will guarantee the sale of GPU capacity for selected AI cloud providers (aka neoclouds) over several years. In return, NVIDIA will share in revenues earned above the guaranteed, or backstop, level. … “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a re\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 8 - e252 [fact; NVIDIA (stated), reported by Futuriom; original source Sharon AI 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA stated that AI cloud provider Sharon AI, based in Australia, has signed on for the new revenue-sharing/credit-support deal and will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 8 - e253 [fact; NVIDIA (stated), reported by Futuriom; original source Firmus 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA stated that AI cloud provider Firmus, based in Indonesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs, under the new revenue-sharing/credit-support deal.\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 8 - e285 [third_party_view; moomoo.com (summarizing a BofA research report); original source Bank of America (BofA) ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] BofA estimates Nvidia's total ecosystem investments — across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others — at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow, framing the 'is Nvidia financing its own demand?' question as still manageable in scale.\n    Quote: Strategic investments across OpenAI, Anthropic, Intel, CoreWeave, Nebius, Lumentum, Coherent, Marvell and others also raise a fair question: is Nvidia financing its own demand? BofA’s answer is that the scale still looks manageable. It estimates total ecosystem investments at around $65 billion, less than 35% of CY2026E free cash flow and about 17% of CY2027E free cash flow.\n\n\nJ 9 - e299 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source Vivek Arya (Bank of America) ; from gnews:86c243945145ed901feb26cf 2026-07-09] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya, in a note this week, listed four concerns driving Nvidia's sell-off: gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n    Quote: Several concerns are fueling the sell-off, Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya wrote in a note this week. They include gross margin pressure from higher memory costs, competition from custom ASIC chips, crowded investor ownership, and unproductive use of cash in vendor financing rather than stronger buybacks/dividends.\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); seen=set();\nfor x in p[12:]:\n for m in re.finditer(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]):\n  k=m.group(1)\n  if k not in seen: seen.add(k); print(\\\"J\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],m.group(0))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); seen=set();\nfor x in p[12:]:\n for m in re.finditer(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- (e'\"\\\\d+) \\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",x[\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]):\n  k=m.group(1)\n  if k not in seen: seen.add(k); print(\\\"J\\\",x[\\\"judgement\\\"],m.group(0))'\"","aggregated_output":"J 13 - e84 [third_party_view; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Big four AI hyperscalers (aggregate spending plan) ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] The article states that the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\n    Quote: The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year.\n\nJ 13 - e85 [party_statement; fool.com (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:92c7e54d38dc2916aa185568 2026-07-04] Nvidia projects that the big four AI hyperscalers' data center capital expenditures could reach $1 trillion in calendar 2027 (next year), up from the roughly $650 billion planned for 2026.\n    Quote: Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion.\n\nJ 13 - e52 [third_party_view; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom's Hardware reported that local opposition to data centers — with 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center in their backyard — led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\n\n\nJ 14 - e119 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley Research estimates that Nvidia 's Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 rack will cost hyperscale cloud providers around $7.8 million per unit, up from roughly $4 million for the current GB300 NVL72 generation.\n\nJ 14 - e120 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and about $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume, and notes the GPUs are not the driver of the rack cost increase.\n    Quote: The GPUs themselves are not the driver of the increase. The bank estimates Nvidia will charge about $55,000 per Rubin GPU and $5,000 per Vera CPU when sold in volume.\n\nJ 14 - e122 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source Morgan Stanley Research ; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Morgan Stanley Research notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the Vera Rubin rack price down to around $6.7 million, illustrating that procurement structure can materially move the per-rack number.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley notes that a hyperscaler self-sourcing certain memory modules could bring the rack price down to around $6.7 million, a reminder that procurement structure now materially moves the number.\n\nJ 14 - e130 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f 2026-07-06] NVIDIA management, on its most recent quarterly conference call, forecast that hyperscaler spending will top $1 trillion in calendar 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026, as reported by Fool.com's Keithen Drury.\n    Quote: During its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 14 - e150 [party_statement; Futurum Group; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\n\nJ 14 - e162 [third_party_view; Allen Harris, The Berkshire Edge; original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512 2026-07-06] Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031, covering chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking and other infrastructure.\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale.\n\nJ 14 - e165 [third_party_view; autonocion.com, citing Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] More than 75 US data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed so far in 2026, according to a running tally at Tom's Hardware cited in the article, and polling in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built near their home.\n    Quote: More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at Tom’s Hardware . Polling covered in the same reporting has 7 out of 10 Americans saying they don’t want one built anywhere near their home.\n\n\nJ 15 - e204 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:f8191f5bb960d79e47c73d6c 2026-07-07] Nvidia claims the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data center capital expenditures next year (2027), up from around $650 billion planned this year (2026), as reported by the article. Both are forward projections/plans, not disclosed actual results.\n    Quote: This year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 15 - e213 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source fool.com ; from gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e 2026-07-07] The article states that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, and next year that figure could exceed $1 trillion, which would raise demand for memory chips as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e84; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 15 - e219 [fact; 24/7 Wall St (Danielle Liverance); original source Google (Alphabet) ; from gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995 2026-07-07] Google reported that Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and guided 2026 capital expenditure up to $190 billion.\n    Quote: Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion.\n\n\nJ 16 - e256 [fact; Bloomberg (reported), via Futuriom; original source Meta 2026-07-01; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Bloomberg reported on July 1 that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models; the article frames this as a possible sign that a major NVIDIA customer's GPU demand (Meta had planned to buy \"millions\" of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier in the year) may be shifting toward reselling spare capacity.\n    Quote: On July 1, the same day NVIDIA announced its new revenue-sharing model, Bloomberg reported that Meta is setting up a cloud-based business selling AI compute and models. If Meta, which planned to buy “millions” of GPUs from NVIDIA earlier this year, now doesn’t need those chips, what does that say about AI demand?\n\nJ 16 - e259 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source IES Holdings ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] IES Holdings reported for its March quarter that Communications segment revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, attributed by the company to continued strong demand in the data center market, and Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% to $192.4 million; total backlog rose to $3.86 billion, a 62% increase since the end of fiscal 2025, driven by customer demand and added capacity.\n    Quote: The March quarter revealed a lot in three data points. Communications revenue rose 35% year over year to $367.7 million, which the company attributed to “continued strong demand in the data center market,” adding that “the recent capital investments we have made have positioned us well to respond to that demand and deliver solutions to our customers.” … Infrastructure Solutions revenue jumped 64% \n\nJ 16 - e260 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services reported Q1 2026 revenue of $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 versus $2.03 consensus (a 31.88% beat) and a record backlog of $48.5 billion; management raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $34.70–35.20 billion and adjusted EPS of $13.55–14.25 and set out a path to more than double adjusted EPS by 2030.\n    Quote: Q1 2026 was a blowout. Revenue hit $7.87 billion, up 26.3% year over year, adjusted EPS of $2.68 beat the $2.03 consensus by 31.88%, and backlog rocketed to a record $48.5 billion. Management raised full-year 2026 guidance to a range of $34.70 billion to $35.20 billion in revenue and $13.55 to $14.25 in adjusted EPS. CEO Duke Austin laid out the long game: a path to “more than doubling our adjuste\n\nJ 16 - e261 [party_statement; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Quanta Services (CEO Duke Austin) ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Quanta Services CEO Duke Austin estimated a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation and large-load demand, framing the opportunity behind the AI infrastructure build-out.\n    Quote: Earl Duke Austin, CEO of specialty contractor Quanta, already attached a number on the opportunity: a $2.4 trillion total addressable market through 2030 from converging utility, generation, and large-load demand.\n\nJ 16 - e262 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Comfort Systems USA ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Comfort Systems USA reported Q1 2026 revenue of $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year with organic growth of 51%, diluted EPS of $10.51 versus $6.81 consensus (a 54.44% beat), and backlog of $12.45 billion versus $6.89 billion a year earlier; data center and technology infrastructure represents roughly 45% of company revenue and management says demand still exceeds supply.\n    Quote: The numbers back him up. Q1 2026 revenue reached $2.87 billion, up 56.5% year over year, with organic growth of 51%. Diluted EPS of $10.51 obliterated the $6.81 consensus by 54.44%, and backlog climbed to $12.45 billion, nearly double the $6.89 billion of a year earlier. … Data center and technology infrastructure now accounts for roughly 45% of company revenue, and management says demand still ex\n\nJ 16 - e263 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source EMCOR Group ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] EMCOR Group reported Q1 2026 diluted EPS of $6.84 versus $5.90 consensus (a 15.85% beat), record Remaining Performance Obligations of $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and raised full-year 2026 guidance to revenue of $18.50–19.25 billion with EPS of $28.25–29.75; its U.S. Electrical Construction segment revenue grew 33.1% in Q1 2026 and Mechanical Construction added 28.8%.\n    Quote: Three data points from Q1 2026 do the work. Diluted EPS of $6.84 beat the $5.90 consensus by 15.85%, Remaining Performance Obligations hit a record $15.62 billion, up 32.9% year over year, and management raised full-year 2026 guidance to $18.50 billion to $19.25 billion in revenue with EPS of $28.25 to $29.75. … Its U.S. Electrical Construction segment posted 33.1% revenue growth in Q1 2026 while \n\nJ 16 - e264 [fact; 247wallst.com (Gerelyn Terzo); original source Sterling Infrastructure ; from gnews:abfc64ce8fc333f13d06a757 2026-07-08] Sterling Infrastructure reported Q1 2026 revenue up 91.59% to $825.7 million, E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue up 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin, and EPS of $3.59 above consensus; management raised full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40–19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\n    Quote: Then came the math. Q1 2026 EPS of $3.59 crushed the consensus by a double-digit percentage, revenue jumped 91.59% to $825.7 million, and E-Infrastructure Solutions revenue rocketed 174% year over year to $597.7 million at a 23.5% adjusted operating margin. Management raised the full-year guidance to adjusted diluted EPS of $18.40 to $19.05, implying 72% growth at the midpoint.\n\nJ 16 - e275 [third_party_view; Investing.com; original source Bank of America (Vivek Arya) 2026-07-08; from gnews:266c882eea8c686ee7aafac7 2026-07-08] Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya argued that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have existed for years, yet Nvidia's GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015; he also said Nvidia's sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\n    Quote: On ASIC competition, Arya pointed out that Google's TPU, Amazon's Trainium and Meta's MTIA have all existed for years, \"yet NVDA GPU revenue has grown approximately 700 times since 2015.\" Sales to hyperscalers rose 115% year-on-year, nearly twice cloud capex growth.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e286; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n\nJ 17 - e374 [third_party_view; TIKR.com (citing TD Cowen analyst Joshua Buchalter); original source TD Cowen ; from gnews:c675b7c2548b02233ee7b05c 2026-07-10] TD Cowen (analyst Joshua Buchalter), cited by TIKR.com, said computing capacity is genuinely tight right now: rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices, which the firm reads as demand outpacing supply. No figures or specific period are given.\n    Quote: TD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. … Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices. … All of that points to demand outpacing supply.\n\n\nJ 18 - e389 [party_statement; Fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:07cf5835d106467db5448c13 2026-07-12] Nvidia told investors in the prior quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year (calendar 2027), according to Fool.com.\n    Quote: However, Nvidia told investors last quarter that it expects AI hyperaccelerator spending to rise to over $1 trillion next year.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 18 - e399 [party_statement; NVIDIA; original source AWS (plan), reported by NVIDIA ; from gnews:8faf69f198e10f14aae67621 2026-07-12] NVIDIA said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs in 2027 to 2028.\n    Quote: Nvidia said in August 2026 that AWS plans to deploy 2 million additional Nvidia GPUs in 2027 to 2028 .\n\n\nJ 19 - e466 [party_statement; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms raised its 2026 capital expenditure plan to a range of $125 billion to $145 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\n    Quote: Meta raised 2026 capex to $125 to $145 billion\n\nJ 19 - e467 [fact; Alex Sirois, 24/7 Wall St.; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:221f52333c8f5e936e515c4d 2026-07-13] Meta Platforms launched Meta Compute, a service that rents its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises, a step 24/7 Wall St. says converts part of its AI capex into a recurring revenue stream.\n    Quote: then quietly launched Meta Compute, a service renting its excess internal AI capacity to external enterprises\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e256; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\nJ 19 - e469 [third_party_view; Rich Duprey (247wallst.com); original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:e0e5ab6c2aced8d5a6a688a5 2026-07-13] Morgan Stanley forecasts that Google, Amazon, Microsoft and Meta will spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year (calendar 2027), adding 19.5 gigawatts of incremental compute capacity, versus roughly 6.7 GW the four added in 2025; Google alone will add 6.8 GW.\n    Quote: The analyst says Google, Amazon, Microsoft, and Meta are forecast to spend over $1 trillion on AI infrastructure next year, adding 19.5 gigawatts (GW) of incremental compute capacity. In 2025, they added roughly 6.7 GW. Google alone will add 6.8 GW, or more than the hyperscalers did combined two years ago.\n\n\nJ 20 - e474 [third_party_view; Justin Pope (The Motley Fool); original source Goldman Sachs ; from gnews:84e90d3da2d3779da0e7bdb0 2026-07-14] Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion in 2026 to $1.13 trillion by 2031, as cited by the Motley Fool's Justin Pope. The figure is a broad AI-compute-spending aggregate, not the four hyperscalers' disclosed data center capital expenditure.\n    Quote: Goldman Sachs estimates that AI compute spending will grow from approximately $494 billion this year to $1.13 trillion by 2031.\n\nJ 20 - e501 [party_statement; Vandita Jadeja, 247wallst.com; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:1e1fa5a194198eaf05d90c9e 2026-07-14] Meta has a $145 billion infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon, cited as a competitive threat to NVIDIA.\n    Quote: The Reddit crowd has fixated on competitive threats, from DeepSeek’s rumored in-house chip to Meta’s $145B infrastructure budget aimed partly at custom silicon.\n\nJ 20 - e537 [fact; ebc.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] Amazon recently borrowed another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt, as Wall Street demands proof of return on hyperscaler AI capital expenditure, according to the EBC article.\n    Quote: Amazon recently made headlines by borrowing another $25 billion, causing a minor selloff in AI-related debt .\n\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); print(re.search(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- e79 '\"\\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",p[2][\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]).group(0)); print(json.dumps(p[14][\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; p=json.load(open(\\\"/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun2/packet-b.json\\\")); print(re.search(r\\\"(?ms)\"'^- e79 '\"\\\\[.*?(?=\"'^- e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[|\"'^'\"\\\\[Current conclusions)\\\",p[2][\\\"request_as_sent\\\"]).group(0)); print(json.dumps(p[14][\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))'\"","aggregated_output":"- e79 [fact; mlq.ai; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] NVIDIA unveiled on July 1, 2026 a financing vehicle called the \"AI Compute Partnership\" under which it acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments: it guarantees a floor utilization rate and, if a partner's customer demand falls short, commits to rent the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, while taking a recurring share of cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. NVIDIA described the model in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress as providing \"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\"; the revenue-share percentage was not disclosed and NVIDIA declined to provide further details.\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . … Nvidia described the model as providing \"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\" in a blog post co-\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n\n{\"rung\": 5, \"rung_label\": \"$1000 billion or more\", \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's stated forecast that the big four AI hyperscalers will spend more than $1 trillion on data-center capex in 2027; this reference is rung 5.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"More than $1 trillion in 2027, based on NVIDIA's forecast against about $650 billion planned for 2026—growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The forecast falls in rung 5, and the supplied delay evidence does not quantify a reduction to this aggregate forecast.\", \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's forecast of more than $1 trillion for 2027, which is rung 5. The implied outcome remains above $1 trillion: that forecast compares with about $650 billion planned for 2026, implying growth of more than roughly 54% year over year. The reported $130 billion of blocked or delayed projects is a meaningful downside risk, but it is the value of projects across the US—not a quantified reduction to 2027 spending by the largest AI hyperscalers—so it cannot be deducted dollar for dollar or shown to take the forecast below $1 trillion. Higher rack costs and Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance are supportive context, but neither independently quantifies 2027 aggregate spending. The NVIDIA segment-revenue dependency has no judged conclusion to constrain this estimate.\", \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied outcome remain above $1 trillion, but the current evidence adds the $130 billion valuation for delayed or blocked projects and Google's $190 billion 2026 capex guidance. These alter the supporting facts and downside context, without quantifying a sufficient 2027 hyperscaler-spending reduction to change the rung.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"judgement\":1,\"verdict\":\"INSUFFICIENT\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"none\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e12 and e14 describe contingent support and planned capacity but provide no amount or comparable that makes below $5 billion more likely than another rung.\"}\n{\"judgement\":2,\"verdict\":\"INSUFFICIENT\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"none\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e34 specifies the agreement's duration and capacity but does not supply a backstop amount or a comparable that favors a dollar rung.\"}\n{\"judgement\":3,\"verdict\":\"INSUFFICIENT\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"none\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e37 describes $30 billion already spent, while the remaining evidence does not size the outstanding backstops or favor a rung.\"}\n{\"judgement\":4,\"verdict\":\"INSUFFICIENT\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"does_not_change_the_rung\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e35 is a separate Digital Alpha facility, not a disclosed comparable from NVIDIA's backstop program, and the remaining evidence does not favor a dollar rung.\"}\n{\"judgement\":5,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"changes_the_rung\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e37 describes $30 billion already spent, while e35 is a separate facility, so neither establishes the outstanding amount used to move to rung 4.\"}\n{\"judgement\":6,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":4,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"The answer's approximately $25.6 billion estimate falls in rung 4, not its selected rung 1.\"}\n{\"judgement\":7,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e114 and e115 support sizing Firmus from the disclosed Sharon deal, producing an estimate within rung 4.\"}\n{\"judgement\":8,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"none\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"Scaling the e114 backstop by the two projects' GPU capacities gives an estimate within rung 4.\"}\n{\"judgement\":9,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"does_not_change_the_rung\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e243 describes a preference-share investment, not an established outstanding financing commitment, but excluding $500 million leaves the estimate in rung 4.\"}\n{\"judgement\":10,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":4,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"does_not_change_the_rung\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"The approximately $26.1 billion implied outcome falls in rung 4, not rung 1, and e243 does not establish the preference shares as an outstanding commitment.\"}\n{\"judgement\":11,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"does_not_change_the_rung\",\"arithmetic\":\"right\",\"reference\":\"holds\",\"implied_outcome\":\"holds\",\"why\":\"e243 does not establish the $500 million preference-share investment as an outstanding commitment, but removing it leaves the estimate in rung 4.\"}\n{\"judgement\":12,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"rung_the_evidence_points_to\":null,\"reasoning_flaw\":\"does_not_change_the_rung\",\"arith